3522: \begin{proposition}[Formal-source gate instantiation]
3523: \label{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}
3524: For the current presentation package, the Lean-facing assertions in rows
3525: \path{pc3} and \path{pc4} of Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} are accepted
3526: only as bounded contextual interfaces, not as accepted formal-source claims.  The
3527: accepted certificate surface is the traceability row $a_1$, the fake-extension
3528: boundary row $a_5$, Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record},
3529: \path{source_interface_record.json},
3530: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and
3531: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}.  These records certify that the
3532: short note may mention the mathlib-free design, marker-audit, and axiom-purity
3533: audit vocabulary only as source-interface context and only with the evidence
3534: boundary stated in rows \path{pc3} and \path{pc4}.  They do not certify a fresh
3535: Lean build, a current full-source marker audit, an absence-of-axioms theorem, or
3536: an accepted axiom-purity pass for the whole newmath tree.  Conversely, a
3537: Lean-facing sentence in this package can become a current accepted formal-source
3538: claim exactly by adding an accepted formal-source certificate row with build-gate
3539: and axiom-purity-gate pass records; if the sentence asserts freshness or currency
3540: of an execution, the row must also include the command, source commit or local
3541: state, environment, exit code, and log path required for command-run evidence.
3542: With the present records, every stronger reading of rows \path{pc3} and
3543: \path{pc4} has a minimal bad subrecord of missing formal-source gates or missing
3544: command-run fields.
3545: \end{proposition}
3546: 
3547: \begin{proof}
3548: By the dispatch rule in the operational interface, an accepted formal-source
3549: claim would have claim kind \emph{formal-source} and therefore would require
3550: pass records for both the build gate and the axiom-purity gate.  The current
3551: acceptance ledger has exactly the six rows $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ displayed in
3552: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}; none has claim kind
3553: \emph{formal-source}.  The machine-readable schema mirrors this fact: its
3554: \path{claim_kind_domain} includes \path{formal-source}, and its
3555: \path{gate_dispatch.formal-source} field names \path{build} and
3556: \path{axiom-purity}, but the current pass-record ledger uses $a_1$ for
3557: traceability, $a_5$ for rejected stronger discovery/theorem-content claims, and
3558: contains no accepted formal-source row with build and axiom-purity pass records.
3559: 
3560: It follows that the only positive certificate available for rows \path{pc3} and
3561: \path{pc4} is contextual.  Row $a_1$ supplies inventory and marker-existence
3562: records for the pinned source-interface snapshot, so the paper may say which
3563: Lean-related source-interface vocabulary is being imported.  Row $a_5$ supplies
3564: the boundary that file churn, compilation, or a fake extension is not theorem
3565: growth or discovery, so the paper may explain why formal-looking material is not
3566: promoted without its gate records.  The certificate schema and primary-claim
3567: inventory make the boundary checkable by naming the absent stronger level: a
3568: formal-source acceptance would need build and axiom-purity pass records, and a
3569: command-run version would additionally need command, source commit, environment,
3570: exit code, and log path.  Since those fields are absent from the current package,
3571: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} rejects the stronger
3572: reading by a finite missing-gate or evidence-level subrecord.  The proposition
3573: therefore certifies exactly the bounded contextual Lean interface and no fresh
3574: Lean build or full-source axiom audit.
3575: 
3576: For the converse boundary, suppose a revised package did add a formal-source
3577: certificate row for one of these sentences.  By the deterministic dispatch rule,
3578: the necessary gate set for the formal-source component is precisely
3579: \(\{\texttt{build},\texttt{axiom-purity}\}\), so pass records for those two gates are
3580: sufficient for the finite schema verifier as far as the formal-source component is
3581: concerned, provided the other coordinates of the record meet the same evidence,
3582: promotion, discovery, and boundary checks as any accepted claim.  If the revised
3583: sentence also says that the build or audit was freshly executed for the current
3584: package, Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} adds exactly the
3585: five command-run fields.  Therefore the present package is not merely silent
3586: about currency: it is a complete negative instantiation of the formal-source
3587: gate, and currency can be claimed only by supplying the missing finite rows.\end{proof}
3588: 
3589: \begin{theorem}[Formal-source promotion obstruction]
3590: \label{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction}
3591: In the current presentation package, no manuscript sentence about the Lean/BEDC
3592: source interface can be promoted from bounded contextual interface evidence to an
3593: accepted formal-source claim unless the certificate is enlarged by an accepted
3594: formal-source row containing build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records.  If a
3595: fresh execution reading is also asserted, the certificate must additionally
3596: contain the command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log
3597: path required for command-run evidence.  With the present six-row package ledger,
3598: every such promotion attempt has a finite bad subrecord and is rejected by the
3599: schema verifier.
3600: \end{theorem}
3601: 
3602: \begin{proof}
3603: The dispatch table sends a formal-source claim to the two gates named in
3604: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}: build and
3605: axiom-purity.  Therefore any accepted formal-source version of rows \path{pc3}
3606: or \path{pc4} would need a certificate whose kind set contains
3607: \emph{formal-source} and whose pass-record field $R$ contains one pass record for
3608: each of these two gates.  By
3609: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the current package has
3610: exactly the accepted certificates $a_1,\ldots,a_6$.  None has claim kind
3611: \emph{formal-source}; none contains build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records;
3612: and the only positive Lean-facing surface is the bounded traceability and
3613: source-interface context identified in
3614: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}.
3615: 
3616: If the promoted reading also asserts a fresh Lean build, current full-source
3617: marker audit, full-source axiom audit, or command-run theorem-source check, then
3618: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} requires the command-run
3619: tuple: command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log
3620: path.  The boundary fields in Tables~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
3621: and~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory} state that no such command-run tuple is
3622: present for the Lean/BEDC source theorem semantics.  Thus a formal-source
3623: promotion fails check~(iii) of the finite verifier by missing required gate pass
3624: records; a fresh-execution promotion fails check~(vi) by unsupported command-run
3625: escalation.  In either case Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
3626: extracts the failed fields as a finite bad subrecord.  Hence the current package
3627: keeps the Lean/BEDC material at the declared bounded contextual level unless new
3628: formal-source and command-run evidence rows are actually added.\end{proof}
3629: 
3630: \begin{theorem}[Evidence-level overclaim obstruction]
3631: \label{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}
3632: Fix the certificate schema, dispatch function, and evidence-level vocabulary of
3633: this paper.  Let $c$ be a load-bearing claim whose accepted certificate has
3634: level path-verified and whose boundary field $B$ records that no command-run
3635: assertion is made.  Then every stronger reading of $c$ that asserts any of the
3636: following additional conclusions is rejected by the finite schema verifier:
3637: \begin{enumerate}
3638: \item external gate soundness for an arbitrary implementation of the workflow;
3639: \item Lean correctness, full-source rebuild success, or axiom-purity beyond the
3640: recorded formal-source gate fields;
3641: \item end-to-end external semantic validity of artifact data beyond the finite
3642: manifest and evaluator surfaces named in $E$ and $R$;
3643: \item a fresh daemon run, full-source rerun, Rule110 or GroundCompiler rerun, or
3644: journal or workshop acceptance; or
3645: \item bundle-backed reviewability when neither \path{review_bundle/} nor an
3646: archival equivalent carrying the same finite records is in the submitted
3647: artifact boundary.
3648: \end{enumerate}
3649: More precisely, such a stronger reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of
3650: one of the five types in
3651: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  Hence the accepted
3652: claim is rigid at its declared evidence level: path-verified certificate records
3653: cannot be promoted by prose into command-run evidence, semantic validation,
3654: formal-source correctness, or external acceptance.
3655: \end{theorem}
3656: 
3657: \begin{proof}
3658: Let $c^+$ be one of the stronger readings.  It has the same displayed manuscript
3659: sentence as $c$ but a larger claim-kind set, a stronger evidence level, or a
3660: larger submitted-artifact boundary requirement.  The verifier does not inspect
3661: rhetorical emphasis; by Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
3662: it inspects only the finite tuple
3663: $\mathcal C=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ and the finite dispatch table.
3664: 
3665: If $c^+$ asserts external gate soundness for an arbitrary implementation, then
3666: it is not merely the finite certificate-construction claim accepted by
3667: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  It needs pass records showing
3668: that the relevant implementation ran every gate in
3669: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$.  Those records are not supplied by the
3670: path-verified certificate for $c$, so the minimal bad subrecord is a missing
3671: pass-record obstruction of type~2.  If $c^+$ asserts Lean correctness,
3672: full-source rebuild success, or axiom-purity, then it is a formal-source or
3673: command-run claim.  Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation} and
3674: Theorem~\ref{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction} identify the required
3675: build, axiom-purity, and command-run fields; when they are absent, the minimal
3676: bad subrecord is type~2 for missing formal-source gates or type~5 for command-run
3677: escalation.
3678: 
3679: If $c^+$ asserts end-to-end semantic validity of artifacts beyond the named
3680: manifest and evaluator surfaces, then it has an artifact component not covered
3681: by the finite status and limitation-ledger records in $R$.  The current
3682: acceptance and certificate ledgers display only the bounded artifact row $a_6$:
3683: its evidence surface is the Rule110 status file, limitation ledgers,
3684: paper-data reference, and review-bundle manifest, and its boundary field says
3685: that no fresh artifact rerun is asserted.  A stronger artifact claim therefore
3686: lacks an assigned artifact pass record, giving type~2,
3687: or asserts a fresh evaluator run without command-run fields, giving type~5.  The
3688: same argument covers a fresh daemon run, full-source rerun, and Rule110 or
3689: GroundCompiler rerun: each is a command-run or artifact-strength escalation and
3690: is rejected by the missing gate or command-run boundary fields.
3691: 
3692: If $c^+$ asserts journal or workshop acceptance, then its acceptance-entry field
3693: would have to contain an editorial or venue acceptance record.  The operational
3694: notion of acceptance entry in this paper is internal and submission-local; the
3695: current ledger contains no external editorial acceptance row.  Thus the stronger
3696: reading either lacks the required route/submission pass record, giving type~2,
3697: or lacks the relevant external evidence surface, giving type~1.  Finally, if a
3698: bundle-backed claim is made while no review bundle or archival equivalent lies
3699: inside the submitted artifact boundary, the evidence item named by the
3700: certificate is absent from $E$ at the declared level, so the minimal bad
3701: subrecord is type~1.  These cases exhaust the displayed stronger readings.
3702: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} then gives the stated
3703: classification and proves rigidity at the declared evidence level.\end{proof}
3704: 
3705: \begin{theorem}[Finite-witness manifest-bounded record interface]
3706: \label{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}
3707: This theorem is a manifest-bounded finite-record interface instance of
3708: Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}: it records only the
3709: bounded manifest/evaluator coordinates that may be checked, and it rejects
3710: stronger artifact readings unless the package supplies the additional finite
3711: records required for them, such as semantic-validation pass records or fresh
3712: rerun logs.  The interface domain is the finite set
3713: \[
3714: \mathcal M_{a_6}=\{\text{manifest entry},\text{local paths},
3715: \text{reviewable snapshots},\text{boundary field},
3716: \text{status pass row},\text{limitation-ledger pass row}\}.
3717: \]
3718: No Rule110 or GroundCompiler property outside $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ is asserted.
3719: 
3720: For the current presentation package, assuming the named review-bundle records
3721: are supplied as supplemental material or by an accepted archive-equivalent
3722: surface, the finite-witness assertions in rows
3723: \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and \path{pc11} of
3724: Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} have the following bounded interface.
3725: The assertions printed in this source are manuscript-source assertions about the
3726: allowed finite-record reading; the path checks against the status files,
3727: limitation ledgers, and machine-readable manifest are supplemental-record
3728: assertions conditional on the named \path{review_bundle/} records being submitted
3729: and matching those paths.
3730: Row \path{pc5} is contextual: it states the evidence discipline that finite
3731: witnesses are bound by manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, and substrate
3732: contracts rather than by Lean alone.  Rows \path{pc6} and \path{pc11} are
3733: accepted only as the bounded artifact lesson $a_6$.  Their named manifest and
3734: evaluator surfaces are \path{manifest:case_evidence[3]}, its
3735: \path{local_paths}, \path{reviewable_snapshots}, and \path{boundary} fields,
3736: the \path{status} and \path{limitation-ledger} pass records for $a_6$ in
3737: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
3738: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_STATUS.md},
3739: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_collision_audit_findings.md},
3740: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_exhaustiveness_audit_findings.md},
3741: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_papers_research_report.md}.
3742: Thus the Rule110/GroundCompiler statement is a finite manifest-bounded artifact
3743: status and limitation-ledger claim, not a claim of Rule110 universality, a fresh
3744: evaluator run, a dynamic behavior theorem, or an unbounded artifact validation.
3745: Its classification is path-complete only for the named finite records in
3746: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  Moreover this bounded interface is invariant under
3747: manifest-record equivalence: any two review states
3748: that agree on the named manifest entry, reviewable snapshots, pass-record rows,
3749: and boundary field receive the same publication certificate from this package.
3750: Therefore no additional Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact property can be derived
3751: from the current certificate unless it is already expressible as one of those
3752: finite manifest-bounded fields or a new artifact/command-run certificate is
3753: added.  In particular, any stronger Rule110 or GroundCompiler reading asserting a
3754: fresh evaluator outcome, semantic validation, dynamic behavior, collision-audit
3755: rerun, exhaustiveness rerun, or universality-level artifact fact requires fresh
3756: evaluator logs with command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path;
3757: manifest rows and historical snapshots alone do not supply such a claim.  The
3758: theorem is exactly a finite-record boundary theorem for the artifact
3759: row $a_6$ in the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}: it preserves the
3760: bounded artifact-limitation lesson and classifies all readings requiring further
3761: artifact evidence as outside the current finite certificate.
3762: \end{theorem}
3763: 
3764: \begin{proof}
3765: The finite-witness vocabulary in \path{pc5} is not itself an artifact pass: it
3766: describes which objects would bind a finite substrate claim.  Under the schema
3767: verifier, such a statement becomes load-bearing only when a finite artifact
3768: certificate supplies the assigned artifact gates.  The dispatch function sends
3769: artifact claims to the status and limitation-ledger gates.  In the current
3770: ledger, the only artifact entry is $a_6$, and
3771: Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} gives its evidence surface: the Rule110
3772: status file, collision and exhaustiveness ledgers, paper-data reference, and the
3773: review-bundle manifest.  The same row records the boundary excluding a fresh
3774: \path{make test-collision-audit} or dynamic artifact rerun.  Therefore the only
3775: coordinates available to the artifact certificate are precisely the finite
3776: coordinates collected in $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.
3777: 
3778: The review bundle supplies a finite external-review surface for precisely that
3779: artifact row.  Proposition~\ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}
3780: identifies the Rule110 case manifest entry with four reviewable snapshots: the
3781: status file, the collision-audit findings, the exhaustiveness-audit findings,
3782: and the papers research report.  The current pass-record ledger names the two
3783: gate records used by $a_6$: a \path{status} pass record whose input surface is
3784: the Rule110 status file and paper-data reference, and a
3785: \path{limitation-ledger} pass record whose input surface is the collision and
3786: exhaustiveness audit ledgers.  These are the manifest and evaluator-facing fields
3787: available to the current package, and they are exactly the concrete
3788: representatives of $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  They are finite strings and bundled
3789: snapshots, so Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} can check
3790: them without interpreting generated prose, rerunning an evaluator, or assigning
3791: semantics to Rule110 or GroundCompiler beyond the recorded manifest/evaluator
3792: boundary.
3793: 
3794: No other artifact entry is present.  Therefore any stronger reading of
3795: \path{pc6} or \path{pc11}---for example, that the Rule110 artifact suite was
3796: freshly rerun, that GroundCompiler is semantically validated beyond the manifest
3797: family, or that Lean supplies an unbounded semantic universe for the artifact
3798: boundary---would require a further artifact certificate or command-run fields.
3799: Those fields are absent, and the schema verifier rejects the stronger reading by
3800: a finite missing-record obstruction.  The accepted finite-witness conclusion is
3801: exactly the bounded $a_6$ artifact lesson.
3802: 
3803: It remains to prove the manifest-record invariance assertion.  Let $W$ and $W'$
3804: be two possible review states, for example two submitted bundles or two local
3805: working trees, that agree on the finite coordinates named in the statement: the
3806: manifest entry, the four reviewable snapshots, the two pass-record rows for
3807: $a_6$, and the boundary excluding fresh reruns.  The certificate constructor and
3808: the schema verifier never query any other coordinate of a review state;
3809: equivalently, these are exactly the artifact coordinates of the finite set $F$
3810: from
3811: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  They therefore build the same tuple
3812: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) for $W$ and for $W'$, and
3813: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} returns the same
3814: accepted bounded-artifact decision in both cases.  A dynamic semantic assertion
3815: that could hold in $W$ and fail in $W'$ is not a function of this finite tuple.
3816: It cannot be a consequence of the current publication certificate.  To make such
3817: an assertion load-bearing, the package would have to add a new finite coordinate:
3818: an artifact pass record for the asserted semantic property, or a command-run tuple
3819: recording the relevant evaluator execution.  This proves manifest-record
3820: invariance for the finite-witness manifest interface and shows that the theorem is
3821: only a finite-record boundary result for row $a_6$ and the coordinate set
3822: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$, not an artifact-validation
3823: theorem.\end{proof}
3824: 
3825: \begin{corollary}[Artifact semantic-validity boundary for finite witnesses]
3826: \label{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary}
3827: Let $c$ be a finite-witness artifact claim in the current package whose accepted
3828: certificate uses the artifact row $a_6$, and let
3829: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ be the manifest-bounded coordinate set of
3830: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}.  If a proposed
3831: stronger reading $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ asserts semantic validity of the unbounded
3832: Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact, semantic correctness of a dynamic evaluator, or
3833: artifact validity beyond the exact evaluator contract recorded in
3834: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$, then $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ is not certified by the present
3835: manuscript package.  It becomes a certifiable reading only after the package adds
3836: one of the following finite coordinates:
3837: \begin{enumerate}
3838: \item an artifact pass record whose input surface is the exact evaluator
3839: contract and whose boundary field states the semantic property being checked; or
3840: \item a command-run tuple for the evaluator, including source state, environment,
3841: command, exit code, and log path, together with the manifest row tying that run to
3842: the submitted artifact.
3843: \end{enumerate}
3844: Consequently, the assertions in primary rows \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and
3845: \path{pc11} that finite substrates are bounded by manifest rows, evaluator
3846: outcomes, and substrate contracts are valid only as finite-record interface
3847: assertions.  For the Rule110/GroundCompiler case they do not assert unbounded
3848: semantic validity unless the exact evaluator contract and its assigned pass or
3849: command-run record are present on the submitted support surface.
3850: \end{corollary}
3851: 
3852: \begin{proof}
3853: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface} identifies the complete
3854: finite coordinate set available to row $a_6$: the manifest entry, local paths,
3855: reviewable snapshots, boundary field, status pass row, and limitation-ledger pass
3856: row.  Its proof also shows manifest-record invariance: two review states agreeing
3857: on those coordinates produce the same artifact certificate even if they differ in
3858: unrecorded dynamic or semantic facts.  Therefore semantic validity of an
3859: unbounded Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact is not a function of the accepted tuple
3860: unless the semantic evaluator contract itself appears as one of the submitted
3861: finite coordinates.
3862: 
3863: Equivalently, compare the accepted artifact reading $c$ with the stronger reading
3864: $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ in the preorder of
3865: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  The stronger reading enlarges at
